    <body>
      <sec id="article-29132.s1" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>
<bold>Theories on the Function of Sleep</bold>
</p>
        <p>Sleep is a complex physiologic process that involves far more than simply closing the eyes and becoming inactive. It is an active state of unconsciousness generated by the body in which the brain enters a relative state of rest while remaining responsive primarily to internal stimuli. Despite extensive research, the precise purpose of sleep has not been fully established. Several major theories have been proposed to explain its biological role, including the inactivity theory, energy-conservation theory, restorative theory, and brain-plasticity theory.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>The inactivity theory suggests that sleep evolved as a protective mechanism. According to this concept, organisms that remained inactive during nighttime hours were less likely to experience injury or predation in low-light conditions, creating an evolutionary advantage that favored nocturnal inactivity. The energy conservation theory proposes that sleep functions to reduce metabolic demands during periods when food acquisition or activity would be less efficient. This theory is supported by physiological evidence demonstrating that metabolic rate can decrease by approximately 10% during sleep.</p>
        <p>The restorative theory emphasizes the role of sleep in physiologic recovery and cellular repair. During sleep, many essential biological processes occur, including muscle repair, tissue growth, protein synthesis, and the release of several hormones involved in growth and metabolic regulation. In contrast, the brain plasticity theory highlights the importance of sleep for neural development, reorganization, and adaptive changes in brain structure and function. This concept is supported by the high sleep requirements observed in infants and children, who may require up to 14 hours of sleep per day during critical stages of brain development.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Although these theories provide valuable perspectives on the function of sleep, none fully explain the phenomenon independently. Current understanding suggests that sleep likely serves multiple biological purposes, and a combination of these mechanisms may contribute to its essential role in human health and neurologic function.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r4">[4]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29132.s2" sec-type="Function">
        <title>Function</title>
        <p>
<bold>Sleep Architecture and Neurophysiology</bold>
</p>
        <p>Sleep follows a relatively predictable cyclical pattern consisting of&#x000a0;2 major phases: non-rapid eye movement (NREM) sleep and rapid eye movement (REM) sleep. NREM sleep is subdivided into stages 1 through 3, each representing progressively deeper levels of sleep and distinct patterns of brain activity, muscle tone, and eye movement. As the name suggests, NREM is characterized by an absence of eye movements, whereas REM is characterized by rapid eye movements and is associated with dreaming. Sleep typically begins with a brief NREM stage 1 phase, followed by stages 2 and 3, before transitioning into REM. NREM constitutes approximately 75% to 80% of total sleep, with REM accounting for the remaining 20% to 25%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r6">[6]</xref> This sequence repeats cyclically throughout the night, with the initial cycle lasting 70 to 100 minutes and subsequent cycles averaging 90 to 120 minutes. The proportion of REM progressively increases over the night, reaching up to 30% of later cycles, with most adults experiencing&#x000a0;4 to&#x000a0;5 complete cycles per night.</p>
        <p>NREM stage 1 is the shallowest stage, lasting 1 to 7 minutes, and is characterized by easily arousable sleep with rhythmical alpha waves on the electroencephalogram (EEG) at 8 to 13 Hz. Stage 2 spans approximately 10 to 25 minutes in the first cycle but may comprise up to 50% of total sleep later in the night. EEG in stage 2 demonstrates low-voltage sleep spindles and K-complexes, and current evidence suggests that memory consolidation occurs primarily during this stage. NREM stage 3, or slow-wave sleep, lasts 20 to 40 minutes initially and is marked by high-voltage, slow-wave EEG activity. REM sleep, responsible for dreaming, is distinguished by total voluntary muscle paralysis except for extraocular muscles, a mechanism thought to prevent dream-related neural activity from producing movement. EEG during REM displays sawtooth waveforms, theta waves, and desynchronized slow and alpha waves.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r6">[6]</xref> Patients with nightmare disorder have been shown to exhibit increased relative high alpha and frontocentral increases in high delta power during REM sleep.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r6">[6]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29132.s3" sec-type="Mechanism">
        <title>Mechanism</title>
        <p>
<bold>Physiology of Sleep Regulation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Sleep generation and maintenance are regulated by the interaction of&#x000a0;2 primary neural systems: the homeostatic sleep drive and the circadian rhythm. The homeostatic process reflects the body&#x02019;s accumulating need for sleep, while the circadian rhythm functions as an internal clock that regulates the timing of the sleep&#x02013;wake cycle. Sleep initiation occurs primarily within the ventrolateral preoptic nucleus of the anterior hypothalamus, which inhibits several arousal-promoting centers of the brain, including the tuberomammillary nucleus, lateral hypothalamus, locus coeruleus, dorsal raphe nucleus, laterodorsal tegmental nucleus, and pedunculopontine tegmental nucleus. Hypocretin (orexin) neurons in the lateral hypothalamus facilitate this regulatory process and help stabilize transitions between sleep and wakefulness.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>NREM sleep is characterized by a functional disconnection between the brainstem and higher cortical structures. This process is mediated by hyperpolarizing gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) neurons within the reticular activating system of the thalamus and cortex. Corticothalamic neurons signal the thalamus, resulting in hyperpolarization of thalamic reticular neurons and the generation of synchronized delta wave activity originating from both thalamic reticular and cortical pyramidal neurons. These neurophysiologic changes correspond with the progressive depth observed across NREM stages 1 through 3. In contrast, REM sleep is generated by &#x0201c;REM-on&#x0201d; neurons located in mesencephalic and pontine cholinergic centers. Activation of the pedunculopontine tegmental nucleus and the laterodorsal tegmental nucleus produces desynchronized cortical activity typical of REM sleep. The tonic component of REM sleep is predominantly parasympathetically mediated, whereas the phasic component demonstrates sympathetic activation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Circadian rhythm represents the body&#x02019;s intrinsic timing mechanism that regulates the desire for sleep. This rhythm is controlled by the hypothalamus through the suprachiasmatic nucleus, which receives sensory input from the retina via the retinohypothalamic tract in response to environmental light levels. The human circadian cycle averages approximately 24.2 hours. Melatonin, secreted by the pineal gland, is an important modulator of circadian timing and varies in concentration with light exposure, with levels typically highest at night and lowest during the day. Core body temperature also follows a circadian pattern, generally reaching lower levels in the early morning and higher levels in the evening, further reflecting the body&#x02019;s internal sleep&#x02013;wake regulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r7">[7]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29132.s4" sec-type="Related Testing">
        <title>Related Testing</title>
        <p>
<bold>Polysomnography and Diagnostic Evaluation of Sleep</bold>
</p>
        <p>Polysomnography is the primary diagnostic modality for evaluating sleep physiology and sleep-related disorders. This comprehensive study incorporates multiple physiologic measurements, including electrocardiography, EEG, electrooculography, electromyography, and oxygen saturation monitoring. Polysomnography is not typically used as a routine screening test but rather as a targeted diagnostic tool when clinical evaluation suggests a sleep disorder. The integration of data from each modality provides a comprehensive assessment of a patient&#x02019;s sleep architecture, physiologic function, and potential sleep-related abnormalities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>Electrocardiography measures the electrical activity of the myocardium and is used to detect cardiac abnormalities during sleep, including disturbances in heart rate and rhythm. Electroencephalography involves the noninvasive placement of electrodes on the scalp to measure voltage fluctuations generated by neuronal activity in the brain. The recorded waveforms allow clinicians to determine the stage of sleep and identify neurologic abnormalities that may occur during sleep. Electrooculography evaluates extraocular muscle activity and is particularly useful for distinguishing sleep stages, as minimal eye movements occur during NREM sleep, while rapid eye movements are characteristic of REM sleep. Electromyography measures muscle activity in respiratory and peripheral muscles, allowing detection of abnormal muscle tone, excessive movement, or periodic limb activity during sleep. Oxygen saturation monitoring evaluates the adequacy of respiration during sleep and helps identify episodes of hypoxemia or apnea that may indicate sleep-disordered breathing.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r8">[8]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29132.s5" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>
<bold>Insomnia</bold>
</p>
        <p>This is the generic term for any illness that causes difficulty falling asleep and staying asleep. This is the most common of sleep illnesses and is commonly related to psychological stressors, poor sleeping environments, irregular sleep schedules, or excessive mental, physical, or chemical stimulation.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Obstructive Sleep Apnea</bold>
</p>
        <p>This is an illness in which major pauses in breathing occur during sleep, secondary to an obstructive process, such as airway collapse due to obesity or weak pharyngeal musculature. When the airway collapses, breathing stops, and hypoxia drives the body to awaken out of deep sleep to breathe again. When this occurs regularly at night, restful sleep is not possible. There are 3 degrees of illness with obstructive sleep apnea: Mild, Moderate, and Severe. Mild OSA is characterized by 5 to 14 apnea episodes per hour. Moderate is defined as 15 to 30 apnea episodes per hour. Severe OSA is when there are 30 or more episodes of OSA in an hour. Positive airway pressure therapy for OSA includes Continuous Positive Airway Pressure (CPAP) and Bilevel Positive Airway Pressure (BiPAP). CPAP is constant-pressure ventilation that keeps the airway open, allowing airflow. BiPAP is when positive pressure supplementation is delivered at 2 pressures, providing the splinting benefit of positive pressure and improving lung ventilation compared to CPAP. Also used as therapy are mandibular advancement devices and surgical therapy, such as uvulopalatopharyngoplasty, adenotonsillectomy, and maxillomandibular advancement. Mainstream therapy is positive airway supplementation, not surgical.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Central Sleep Apnea</bold>
</p>
        <p>This illness is similar to obstructive sleep apnea. However, its etiology is related to intrinsic diminution and ultimately failure of the breathing drive or mechanisms during sleep. Such illnesses include congenital central hypoventilation syndrome (the Ondine curse) or congestive heart failure. When breathing is ineffective, the body awakens from deep sleep to correct hypoxia. Treatment consists of CPAP, BiPAP, Adaptive Servo-Ventilation, or medical therapy with acetazolamide or theophylline.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Mixed Sleep Apnea (Complex Sleep Apnea)</bold>
</p>
        <p>It is a combination of obstructive and central sleep apnea symptoms.&#x000a0;This is an illness where patients with symptoms of obstructive sleep apnea develop symptoms of central sleep apnea upon treatment with CPAP therapy during a sleep study.&#x000a0;Treatment is typically very&#x000a0;low-pressure CPAP therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Ghrelin-Leptin Abnormalities</bold>
</p>
        <p>Sleep duration has been found to play a significant role in maintaining body weight. The theory is that an imbalance in the&#x000a0;2 essential hunger-related hormones (Leptin and Ghrelin) is associated with altered sleep habits. Leptin is produced by adipose cells and regulates energy balance by inhibiting the hunger drive. Ghrelin is the counter-regulatory hormone to leptin produced by cholinergic cells in the gastrointestinal tract and is responsible for increasing appetite, thus encouraging the hunger drive. Shorter sleep duration is associated with reduced leptin and elevated ghrelin. These differences in leptin and ghrelin likely lead to increased appetite, possibly explaining the increased BMI observed with sleepless nights. This aberration is commonly associated with OSA because increased BMI is directly related to its development. The treatment here is to sleep consistently 7 to 8 hours per night.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Narcolepsy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Narcolepsy type 1 occurs when&#x000a0;nearly all of the neurons that contain orexin (also called hypocretin) are lost. The mechanism of narcolepsy type 2 is less clear, but it is thought that it may be due to a similar but&#x000a0;less severe loss of orexin neurons. During normal REM sleep, orexin levels decrease, reducing&#x000a0;reticular activating system&#x000a0;activity and promoting atonia. In narcolepsy type 1, the mechanism that separates wake from sleep&#x000a0;becomes unstable without sufficient levels of orexin. The&#x000a0;reticular activating system no longer consistently causes the release of wake-promoting neurotransmitters to the cortex and inconsistently inhibits the ventrolateral preoptic nucleus.&#x000a0;This results in rapid transitions between sleep and wakefulness and allows REM-related phenomena to intrude into wakefulness. This is characterized by episodes of daytime somnolence, cataplexy, and, occasionally, hallucinations throughout the day that extreme emotions may trigger. Many people with narcolepsy also suffer from insomnia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Somnambulism</bold>
</p>
        <p>A state of combined sleep and wakefulness that leads to sleepwalking. Sleepwalking is when a person ambulates or performs actions without consciously controlling the movement or having a memory of the event. This illness is not fully understood, but it has been associated with increased slow-wave sleep and sleep deprivation, and there is evidence that there is a genetic component of inheritance. Mainstay therapy consists of benzodiazepines.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29132.r14">[14]</xref></p>
      </sec>
